ders balanced over her feet. A brass ring fixed to a waist-high backstage rail sits on her right side, clear of the jacket front. Her right hand grips the doubled canvas seam just beyond the ring and draws the corner through it; her left hand holds the folded edge against the near side of the ring, keeping the loop from slipping back. Each forearm leads visibly from its own sleeve to its hand. The gathered corner now hangs as a compact vertical tail beneath the ring, while the free upper canvas still bows in the wind. Its painted blue arch is kinked by the pulled fold, making the change in tension visible. Her gaze rests on the attachment, her lips gently parted as the next knot waits to be made. A chalk stage mark and two folded rehearsal chairs situate this brief repair within the rehearsal space. Photograph the moment from a front three-quarter position at her waist height, in a vertical 4:5 full-length environmental frame with her face, both hands, the ring, skirt folds and boots visible together. Place the moving canvas toward the right edge and leave breathing space around the attachment. Cool late-afternoon skylight reveals the tweed and wool surfaces; a warm rehearsal lamp by the open theatre door gives a small amber edge to her cheek and knit collar. Keep natural skin texture, believable fabric weight and moderate depth of field, with the subject and contact plane crisp and the distant roof parapet softly receding.",
